1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,120
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:13,860
Alright, there's some things that we say as Scout leaders that are taken in exactly the opposite meaning.

3
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:14,740
I'll give you an example.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,500
If, on a camping trip, I say lights out, all quiet,

5
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:27,540
It is heard as it's time to test your flashlight and to talk in a very loud voice.

6
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:29,940
I mean, am I right or am I right?

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,900
Hey, this is podcast number 43..

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,140
Welcome to the Scoutmaster podcast.

9
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:09,300
This is Clark Green.

10
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:13,840
Hey, I found this blog post on Brian Spellman's blog.

11
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:16,240
He's a Scoutmaster in Fishers, Indiana.

12
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,320
He says he's a new Scoutmaster

13
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:24,520
And you know we do a lot of talking about youth leadership and the patrol method and things like that.

14
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:32,380
We talk about our place as leaders and training and trusting and letting the boys lead.

15
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:34,759
That's a tough piece of work.

16
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:37,000
Sometimes you don't see very much.

17
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:39,300
Well, Brian saw something and this is what he wrote on his blog.

18
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:44,720
He says: you know, you think you know when it works, but you really don't.

19
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:49,980
It seems to be going right and everything starts to fall in line, just like they told you at training,

20
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:52,480
And you're positive, it's working.

21
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:59,840
You talk to other leaders and you chat on forums and you post on Twitter about how it's working for you,

22
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:01,100
And then it happens.

23
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,440
You see it and it works.

24
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:12,459
You've been standing on the mountaintop proud of your Scouts, when you are really just on a cloudy ledge and the mist clears and you see the top.

25
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:15,019
This is what happened last night.

26
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:19,180
I've been very proud of my Scouts but man, oh man, did last night take the cake.

27
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:24,299
There were 68 Scouts plus 23 visiting Weevlows. at the meeting last night,

28
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:28,840
Assistant Senior Patrol leaders sat out front and welcomed the Weevlows and directed them where to go,

29
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:29,720
Flags were put out,

30
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:35,960
The meeting started, patrol meetings run, troop white skill demo gave and game played with the Weevlows and I did nothing.

31
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:38,720
Well, not nothing.

32
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:40,840
I talked with the Weevlows parents,

33
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:49,880
I signed off a handbook, signed off an Eagle project, gave a star Scoutmaster conference and later in the evening an Eagle Scoutmaster conference.

34
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:56,780
Finally, I talked to the troop with about 10 minutes left in the meeting just to plea for den chiefs and then a Scoutmaster minute.

35
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:00,060
I talked with my senior patrol leader afterwards.

36
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:03,720
His patrol leader's counsel is doing a great job and the troop is really humming.

37
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:10,720
But for some reason last night illustrated for me just how far they've taken the troop in a few short months and I couldn't be prouder.

38
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,020
I think everybody's had that experience.

39
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:18,300
I mean we have the experience of looking at things and thinking that they're falling apart-

40
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:18,420
Weird.

41
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:21,500
I mean, I have that experience.

42
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:28,480
But there are times when, from your easy chair in the back of the meeting room, you look out,

43
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:30,520
You do have one of those right,

44
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:31,980
An easy chair in the back of the meeting room.

45
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:34,060
I suggest you get one as quickly as possible.

46
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:38,040
You look out and you see that things are really working.

47
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:40,680
This scouting, it really works.

48
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:42,380
It really really does.

49
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:42,980
Thank you, Brian.

50
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,760
I really enjoyed reading that.

51
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:48,220
We talked about hazing last time around.

52
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:50,100
Larry Geiger had this to say:

53
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:59,620
There are some Scoutmasters who, based on their own experience as a boy, think that young men need some shaking up or that they need to be challenged and tested,

54
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,280
They need to run some kind of gauntlet.

55
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:08,579
They may think that they should be tough and demanding or harsh and that they should put their Scouts through some kind of trial.

56
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:11,500
Now they're partly right, but they're misguided.

57
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,780
Scouting is challenging, but without being negative.

58
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:24,880
A Scout's first summer camp or trek to Philmont is a test, a challenge that does shape up a Scout's mental and physical and spiritual makeup.

59
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:31,380
A trip without Mom and Dad at 11 years old is enough all by itself to, you know, be pretty frightening to a new Scout.

60
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:34,820
He surely doesn't need a snipe hunt to do that.

61
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:43,740
Taking a young Scout on his first backpacking trip miles into the woods with nothing but a trail and some blazes to find his way back is really a rite of passage.

62
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:47,540
Surveying a disastrous Sunday morning breakfast and thinking

63
00:04:47,659 --> 00:04:54,020
I'm going to starve to death before Sunday dinner is enough to give a hungry 11-year-old fits.

64
00:04:54,020 --> 00:05:03,919
Walking into the woods with a bunch of Scouts that you don't know and have just met, most of them older and more experienced than you and some of them adult-sized, is intimidating enough.

65
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:12,780
Do Scouting Right, and the young men will talk all their life about how Scouting shaped them up and helped them to grow,

66
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:16,460
How the challenges were very real to them.

67
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:23,039
What we see as normal everyday challenges in Scouting are a rite of passage for most young men

68
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:28,900
And for those young men who look for more challenge. well, we've got a couple of those in our hip pocket too.

69
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:34,760
How about leading, training and inspiring their fellow Scouts to achieve first-class rank?

70
00:05:35,039 --> 00:05:38,140
Or mentor a couple of dozen new Scouts to get first-class rank?

71
00:05:38,219 --> 00:05:39,520
Now there's a challenge for them.

72
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:41,859
Larry, you put it just right.

73
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:45,260
When we do stuff that Scouts do. it's a challenge.

74
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:46,420
It's going to shape them up.

75
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:58,540
It's going to do all those things that we think maybe being harsh or critical or difficult or even some of this hazing stuff might do for them.

76
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:00,480
That's not going to do it for them.

77
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:02,440
We know what's going to do it for them.

78
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:03,780
Scouting is going to do it for them.

79
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,240
So in this podcast I have an email to answer.

80
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:08,640
It's going to take a little while.

81
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:09,840
It's a good email,

82
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:10,480
Good question,

83
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:11,980
And you know what

84
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,720
That's going to be about it this time around.

85
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:15,880
It is really busy around here,

86
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:17,840
Got a lot of irons in the fire,

87
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:19,600
A lot of scouting going on,

88
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:23,400
And I think it's time we get started. shall we

89
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:26,120
Write me a letter,

90
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:28,340
Send it by mail,

91
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:31,480
Email that is. folks,

92
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,140
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

93
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:45,400
This email comes to me from Tim.

94
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,080
Tim says: I've been intently reading your blog now for about six months.

95
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:53,940
I've come to value your insight and embrace your principles.

96
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:56,480
Now I consider myself one of your disciples.

97
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:57,160
Oh, Tim,

98
00:07:00,219 --> 00:07:01,620
No, I appreciate it.

99
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:02,320
Kind words,

100
00:07:02,719 --> 00:07:03,140
Disciple,

101
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:06,520
Putting a little pressure on me there, brother,

102
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:08,660
That's fine, though

103
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:24,480
He goes on to say: as a former Scoutmaster, now Chartered Organization Representative, I want to ask your thoughts about the fulfillment of the leadership requirement, especially as it pertains to the patrol leader and more specifically as it relates to the Eagle candidate.

104
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:34,460
I understand that leadership requirements by BSA standard is fulfilled simply by completing the required tenure. in qualifying for the position of responsibility,

105
00:07:35,640 --> 00:07:50,100
Initially as a new Scoutmaster, I erroneously and ignorantly established a minimum qualifying percentile of participation in troop activities, with the commonly held assumption that this would compel patrol leaders to be more dutiful to their patrols.

106
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:59,040
Fortunately, I became aware, through some research that I did, that this practice was contrary to BSA policy and I immediately stopped it.

107
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:05,580
I recognize that the BSA concludes the participation policy with the following statement:

108
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:12,300
Unit leaders must ensure that he is fulfilling the obligations of his assigned leader position.

109
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:16,640
If he is not, then they should remove the Scout from that position.

110
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:36,559
Now we have a few Scouts that get elected as patrol leaders and then habitually neglect to attend campouts, oftentimes without the backup of an assistant patrol leader in attendance as well, leaving three or four Scout, Tenderfoot and Second Class Scouts without the benefit of the patrol leader that they not only elected but trusted to lead them.

111
00:08:36,559 --> 00:08:44,739
Now, before you retort with the contention that there is something lacking in the program to pique the boys' interest. I assure you this isn't the case.

112
00:08:44,859 --> 00:08:52,060
Our overall participation level is really very high by all age levels and our Patrol Leaders Council controls the troops' program.

113
00:08:52,479 --> 00:09:03,479
These patrol leaders, before accepting the position, are informed that the expectation is for them to attend most activities and campouts and provide the greatest opportunity for them to demonstrate their leadership.

114
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:09,880
We emphasize trust, loyalty and servant leadership in troop leader training.

115
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:20,800
Now I want to stop there for a moment because I will guarantee you, Tim, that most of the people who are listening to this podcast could have written me the same email.

116
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,440
I guarantee it.

117
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:27,120
I know that I have the same situation, but I'm going to keep moving.

118
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:29,380
Tim goes on to say:

119
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:40,200
At present we have a patrol leader aged 14 and a life scout who is three months into his six-month term. so far is one for three on campout attendance.

120
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:47,800
At last night's troop meeting he approached our advancement coordinator with a list of Eagle-required merit badges that he was requesting the names of counselors for

121
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:52,520
The scout, then declared he had a deadline to have his Eagle done by June 1st-

122
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:58,380
His dad's kind of a hard-charging, overachieving military type. and the boy is the exact opposite,

123
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:07,360
The scoutmaster, and I believe that the boy merely took on the patrol leader position, as directed by his dad, as an avenue to fulfilling the requirement for advancement.

124
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:15,180
It seems to me to be asking a lot of the senior patrol leader to burden him with the unpleasant task of removing a scout from his position.

125
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:15,800
Am I wrong?

126
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:22,240
Should we consider these absences to be justification for removal from his position of a patrol leader?

127
00:10:22,579 --> 00:10:28,540
Should he be asked to resign or remove from his position until he can commit to regular attendance?

128
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:37,199
Should he be asked by the senior patrol leader to resign his position of patrol leader and then offered a less demanding position which he can fulfill the responsibilities of?

129
00:10:37,579 --> 00:10:40,859
Should the scout be allowed to continue as patrol leader without consequence?

130
00:10:40,860 --> 00:10:46,700
Do the scouts in his patrol have to suffer from his leadership abandonment?

131
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:48,720
I mean, what remedy do they have?

132
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:52,260
Should they elect a new patrol leader to finish out his term?

133
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:55,660
If their votes elected him, wouldn't they be entitled to recall?

134
00:10:56,280 --> 00:11:02,560
Should the patrol scouts be informed of that prerogative, if it exists, and encouraged to exercise it?

135
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:05,000
Can you help us out here?

136
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:06,600
I mean, what are we doing wrong?

137
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:09,500
I'm more than willing to admit that we're doing something wrong.

138
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:12,400
Tell me why it's broken and how can we fix it?

139
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:18,080
I'm mentoring a very enthusiastic but frustrated, committed and perplexed scoutmaster.

140
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:20,920
This is a perennial dilemma for the both of us.

141
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:27,500
It's easy to see why most troops- albeit incorrectly- hold on to a measurable standard.

142
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:33,400
Well, Tim, it is a pretty familiar story to most scoutmasters, so let's start working on this.

143
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:36,420
Let me answer your last question first,

144
00:11:36,420 --> 00:11:39,439
And that is: what are we doing wrong?

145
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:42,079
Well, you aren't doing anything wrong.

146
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:44,420
What you describe is bound to happen.

147
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:48,479
It's not a malfunction or a symptom of some kind of problem with the way you're presenting the program.

148
00:11:49,339 --> 00:11:59,420
Now, you say this and you correctly quote a piece of BSA policy that talks about active tenure as a scout and active leadership,

149
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:06,360
And it says this: unit leaders must ensure that he's fulfilling the obligations of his assigned leadership position.

150
00:12:06,459 --> 00:12:08,979
If he's not, then they should remove the scout from that position.

151
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,959
And you ask if this is the senior patrol leader's job.

152
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:13,479
Yeah,

153
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:14,500
Yep,

154
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:16,479
The senior patrol leader is the man for the job.

155
00:12:16,579 --> 00:12:18,000
He's always nearing the man for the job,

156
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,620
But nobody's going to get removed from any position

157
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:26,940
And nobody's going to have to go through some burdensome procedural to do this.

158
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:28,840
It's really quite simple.

159
00:12:29,180 --> 00:12:30,760
It's up for the patrol to decide.

160
00:12:31,400 --> 00:12:35,280
You asked a series of questions there, Tim, and I'm going to just go over them one by one.

161
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:42,100
Should we consider these absences to be justification for removal from the position of patrol leader?

162
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:44,480
I think that's an easy answer there.

163
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:44,860
No,

164
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:45,640
No,

165
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:50,640
The scouts in his patrol may think it's a justification to hold a patrol leader election

166
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:56,800
Now, assuming that you don't appoint patrol leaders and you don't step in and remove them either, right?

167
00:12:57,940 --> 00:13:06,300
The next question Tim asks is: should he be asked to resign or removed from his position until he can commit to regular attendance?

168
00:13:06,300 --> 00:13:09,300
And I would say that nobody needs to ask him to resign.

169
00:13:09,579 --> 00:13:12,420
Nobody needs to remove him from his position as a patrol leader.

170
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:14,060
This is up to his patrol.

171
00:13:14,839 --> 00:13:20,260
Should he be asked by the senior patrol leader to resign his position of patrol leader and then offer a less demanding position

172
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:22,920
which he can fulfill the responsibilities of?

173
00:13:23,300 --> 00:13:26,540
Well, if the senior patrol leader sees this as a problem, he'll want to get it fixed,

174
00:13:27,060 --> 00:13:30,040
And I'd ask the senior patrol leader exactly what he wants to do about it

175
00:13:30,079 --> 00:13:32,859
and I would make sure that the senior patrol leader sees it as a problem.

176
00:13:33,579 --> 00:13:35,560
Nobody has to ask anybody to resign.

177
00:13:35,560 --> 00:13:40,680
Should the scout be allowed to continue as patrol leader without consequence?

178
00:13:41,040 --> 00:13:41,579
Well, yeah,

179
00:13:42,199 --> 00:13:42,439
Yep,

180
00:13:42,959 --> 00:13:47,979
But his patrol is going to make the decision of any consequences that there are out there.

181
00:13:48,479 --> 00:13:51,520
Do the scouts in his patrol have to suffer from his leadership abandonment?

182
00:13:51,619 --> 00:13:52,579
What remedy do they have?

183
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:52,839
Yeah,

184
00:13:53,119 --> 00:13:53,939
They have to suffer.

185
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:59,700
I'm laughing, okay, but I understand that this is. you know, I know this is a heartfelt question.

186
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:02,020
But yes, they have to suffer.

187
00:14:02,120 --> 00:14:02,880
It's all part of the game.

188
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:06,760
And if they know that they can elect a new patrol leader anytime they want, they won't suffer for long.

189
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:08,120
Meh,

190
00:14:10,140 --> 00:14:14,100
Should they elect a new patrol leader to finish out his term?

191
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:18,680
If their votes elected him, wouldn't they be entitled to recall?

192
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:24,440
Shouldn't the patrol scouts be informed that that prerogative exists and encouraged to exercise it?

193
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:25,340
And Tim, there you go.

194
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:26,540
You've answered your own questions.

195
00:14:27,960 --> 00:14:29,320
First of all, there's no recall.

196
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:34,680
There's no procedural requirement other than the patrol determining that it's time to elect a new patrol leader.

197
00:14:35,820 --> 00:14:38,900
The term a patrol leader serves is the prerogative of the patrol.

198
00:14:38,900 --> 00:14:44,160
Now, somewhere in your email you mentioned that you have a six-month term for patrol leaders.

199
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:45,420
You know that's fine,

200
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:50,140
But I wouldn't set any kind of artificial term on patrol leadership.

201
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:57,020
I would say that it's the patrol's prerogative to go ahead and have a patrol leader election whenever they want to.

202
00:14:57,079 --> 00:14:57,720
It's their patrol.

203
00:14:58,120 --> 00:14:58,819
It's their leader.

204
00:14:59,480 --> 00:15:07,140
If they select a tenderfoot scout as patrol leader who's 11 years old, I'm going to work with the tenderfoot who's 11 years old.

205
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:13,860
If they select a 14-year-old scout who is a star or life or maybe even eagle, I'm going to work with him.

206
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:15,340
It's their choice.

207
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:17,080
It's what they choose to do.

208
00:15:17,880 --> 00:15:28,120
I will give them a little bit of guidance here and there, make some suggestions about. you know how they should consider candidates for patrol leadership,

209
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:36,660
But boys in a patrol are pretty good at sussing out who is a good leader and who they want to have lead their patrol.

210
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:39,760
I recently watched this happen to a patrol in my troop.

211
00:15:39,960 --> 00:15:42,560
The patrol leader was basically a no-show.

212
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:44,320
He wasn't making calls,

213
00:15:44,420 --> 00:15:48,120
He wasn't responding to any encouragement from me or the senior patrol leader.

214
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:52,660
I spent some time with one of the scouts in his patrol that was dismayed over the situation.

215
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:58,920
I mean he had let it be known that he was unhappy that he had a patrol leader that wasn't showing up.

216
00:16:00,140 --> 00:16:05,000
So you know, I asked him how things were going and what he thought he should do about it,

217
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:06,820
And he really didn't know what to do.

218
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,180
And I asked him when the patrol planned on having another patrol leader election.

219
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:13,699
And he really didn't know.

220
00:16:13,819 --> 00:16:19,780
I mean, he thought that he had to wait for some reason or another or somebody else was going to make the decision as to when they had an election.

221
00:16:19,880 --> 00:16:23,260
I told him they could have a patrol leader election anytime they wanted.

222
00:16:24,300 --> 00:16:26,199
Within a week or two they had a new patrol leader.

223
00:16:27,439 --> 00:16:30,079
Now nobody had to remove Mr No-Show,

224
00:16:31,739 --> 00:16:38,520
Nobody had to sit and talk to him and you know, say that there was a problem and that we needed to fix it or anything like that.

225
00:16:38,979 --> 00:16:40,979
The guy's just got a new patrol leader.

226
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:45,620
Now, obviously this causes us all a little bit of angst.

227
00:16:46,079 --> 00:16:47,579
I mean, it causes me angst.

228
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:53,699
You know you want to fix the problem, but you know that you shouldn't just step in and fix it.

229
00:16:54,099 --> 00:16:56,079
You know that the scouts have a role in it

230
00:16:56,859 --> 00:17:04,740
And all they really need is the knowledge to make the changes that they need to make and the forbearance of adults involved not to make the changes for them.

231
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:10,740
You're also pretty sure this may be your fault.

232
00:17:11,180 --> 00:17:13,240
I know I am when this comes up.

233
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:17,280
I mean, why wouldn't a guy show up to meetings or outings when he was a patrol leader

234
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:19,780
And what's you know, these family trips.

235
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:20,980
What's all this about?

236
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:24,120
Well, it's not our fault.

237
00:17:24,119 --> 00:17:26,659
It is beyond the control of anybody.

238
00:17:27,359 --> 00:17:29,520
It's what a family decides to do.

239
00:17:29,979 --> 00:17:31,459
It's the way life goes,

240
00:17:31,659 --> 00:17:35,300
You know. I mean, at one point you have to have a little serendipity about this type of thing,

241
00:17:35,300 --> 00:17:38,399
And I got to tell you the scout isn't really doing anything wrong.

242
00:17:39,100 --> 00:17:41,000
He is 14 years old,

243
00:17:41,659 --> 00:17:42,800
And that says a lot.

244
00:17:43,260 --> 00:17:43,919
That says a lot.

245
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,740
Now, that doesn't forgive irresponsibility,

246
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:50,159
It doesn't forgive being cavalier about your leadership position or something like that,

247
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:53,140
But it means that he's 14..

248
00:17:54,180 --> 00:17:58,140
And so guiding, mentoring, talking, working through these things with him,

249
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:01,260
That's the best thing to have happen, isn't it?

250
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:08,800
Now, if Tim has read the family situation he describes correctly- and I have no reason to think that you haven't-

251
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:12,879
there's not going to be a whole lot. you can do about that either, and I think you pretty much know that.

252
00:18:13,420 --> 00:18:14,360
I'll offer this, though

253
00:18:14,379 --> 00:18:19,860
What you see as a hard-driving dad is probably not how you or I would do the job of parenting,

254
00:18:19,860 --> 00:18:22,660
But we have to have a little serendipity about that too.

255
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:23,720
We're not going to change it.

256
00:18:24,280 --> 00:18:25,780
Our scouts come to us as they are.

257
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:27,300
Some families are neglectful,

258
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:28,780
Some are over-attentive,

259
00:18:28,780 --> 00:18:32,840
Some are, you know, in the full spectrum. in between,

260
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,680
It's best when we take our scouts as they come to us,

261
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:41,980
We reserve our judgment of how their family is run and we just let them be scouts.

262
00:18:44,100 --> 00:18:51,100
Now, can someone you know, can this hard-driving dad or a hard-driving parent, shortcut the advancement system?

263
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:53,920
Can they lawyer their way through an Eagle badge?

264
00:18:54,560 --> 00:18:54,820
Yeah,

265
00:18:55,180 --> 00:18:56,000
I mean they can.

266
00:18:56,660 --> 00:18:58,379
It happens from time to time

267
00:18:58,780 --> 00:19:01,720
And that's why scouting is kind of a case-by-case affair.

268
00:19:02,180 --> 00:19:09,560
It saddens me that some parents do more than I would like, that they helicopter their son's advancement and the rest of their lives as well.

269
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:13,000
But the integrity of the advancement in scouting does not need my protection.

270
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:15,980
It does just fine on its own, and it has for over a century.

271
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:33,640
So let's say, the way that this situation is resolved is that the patrol in question has a patrol leader election and the scout who has not been attending faithfully stops being the patrol leader.

272
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:37,040
Now he doesn't have leadership tenure towards life or eagle,

273
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:40,580
And dad might hit the ceiling and demand an explanation.

274
00:19:40,580 --> 00:19:47,320
The one he would get from me is that the scouts and the patrol made a decision and I'm not going to second-guess them.

275
00:19:47,360 --> 00:19:52,439
There are plenty of leadership opportunities for your son and he should talk to the senior patrol leader.

276
00:19:52,440 --> 00:20:02,460
But I would guess he would probably need to show up more and make some more effort than he did as a patrol leader if he wants the senior patrol leader to consider him for a position of leadership.

277
00:20:03,820 --> 00:20:08,580
In short, the decision as to who leads them is invested in the scouts.

278
00:20:08,580 --> 00:20:11,860
Adults- I mean adults- really have little or no say in the matter.

279
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:14,939
They vote for their patrol leaders,

280
00:20:15,159 --> 00:20:16,540
They vote for their senior patrol leader.

281
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,220
The senior patrol leader makes some appointments.

282
00:20:19,320 --> 00:20:23,860
I don't think that these positions of responsibility and leadership should be reserved for anybody.

283
00:20:24,699 --> 00:20:31,679
They're open to every scout and the scouts themselves will identify who the most capable leader is.

284
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:34,100
They'll do it almost 100% of the time.

285
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:36,120
Every once in a while, meh, they're not so smart,

286
00:20:36,120 --> 00:20:42,260
But I trust them a great deal to know the scouts that they're working with.

287
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:43,360
They know them better than I do.

288
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:51,419
Our job is to mentor and train whoever they select through a process of developing into a good person, into being a good leader,

289
00:20:52,280 --> 00:20:57,060
And as for evaluating the job that they do, I put that squarely on their shoulders.

290
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:01,520
Before I sign a leadership requirement, I ask a scout to evaluate himself,

291
00:21:01,939 --> 00:21:04,699
And he is usually harder on himself than I will be.

292
00:21:06,120 --> 00:21:10,520
From time to time we're all going to run into the situation you described, Tim.

293
00:21:11,459 --> 00:21:17,239
When they come along, the senior patrol leader and his fellow leaders usually have a better way of sorting things out than we do.

294
00:21:17,860 --> 00:21:22,719
In fact, I'll go so far as to say that we want situations like you described to happen.

295
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:34,520
I mean, what better way of learning leadership could we offer than dealing with real-world difficulties that are actually real, you know, than theoretical

296
00:21:35,860 --> 00:21:38,400
Scouts will really rise to the moment?

297
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:48,820
And this doesn't mean that our patrol leader, with a problem of attending- and things like that is now totally out of the picture- never gets to be a leader again, has to go hang his head in shame.

298
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:51,300
No, that would be our job too,

299
00:21:52,720 --> 00:21:56,960
To go and to say: well, I heard there was a patrol leader election and you're not the patrol leader anymore.

300
00:21:57,100 --> 00:21:57,840
Well, no, sir.

301
00:21:58,120 --> 00:21:59,439
Well, how did that make you feel?

302
00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:02,100
How's you know? what do you think of that?

303
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:08,419
Well, I really didn't have the time to do it the way that I should, and I was feeling kind of bad about it, and I'm glad the guys had the election anyway.

304
00:22:08,919 --> 00:22:10,760
That's one possible thing he might tell you.

305
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:18,920
You know, he could say that he's upset and he didn't think that it was fair and we can talk through that.

306
00:22:19,879 --> 00:22:27,440
But you know everything. every time I get a question like this and every time I start thinking about things that happen in my troop,

307
00:22:27,860 --> 00:22:36,320
I realize how easy it is just to place these things within the responsibility and authority of our youth leadership, and they usually get it sorted out.

308
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:38,160
Scouts are pretty smart people.

309
00:22:38,160 --> 00:22:40,320
Well, thanks for writing, Tim,

310
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:44,000
And if you want to send me an email, I'm going to tell you how just in a moment.

